{"entries":{"chapter-88-study-cancer-and-aging-general-principles|Chapter 88 Study: Cancer and Aging: General Principles":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"epidemiology-amp-aging-cancer-biology|Epidemiology & Aging–Cancer Biology":["u3"],"copy|Cancer is the 2nd leading cause of death after heart disease in the US; >60% of newly diagnosed malignant tumors and 70% of all cancer deaths occur in persons 65+ (p1381).":["u4"],"copy|Aging and neoplasia share the same underlying biological mechanisms (Hallmarks of Cancer / Hallmarks of Aging) but push in opposite directions: aging → reduced proliferation/senescence; neoplasia → accumulated damage → increased proliferation — \"opposite sides of the same coin\" (p1382).":["u5"],"copy|Telomere shortening begins at age 30 and proceeds at ~1%/year loss; loss of telomere function → genetic instability → oncogenic mutation risk (p1383).":["u6"],"copy|Time from a mutated cell to a clinically detectable tumor ≈ 10–30% of a species' maximum lifespan (p1383).":["u7"],"copy|7 proposed mechanisms for rising cancer risk with age: longer carcinogen exposure duration, altered carcinogen susceptibility, decreased DNA repair, oncogene activation/tumor-suppressor loss, telomere shortening, cellular senescence/SASP-driven microenvironment change, decreased immune surveillance (p1383–1384).":["u8"],"screening-amp-presentation|Screening & Presentation":["u9"],"copy|Older adults are simultaneously underscreened (racial/ethnic minorities, <high-school education, below poverty line) and overscreened (routine screening continues even with <5-year life expectancy or severe nursing-home disability, risking net harm from work-up) (p1384).":["u10"],"copy|New Mexico study of 800 patients older than 65: only 29% were asymptomatic at diagnosis; 48% presented within 2 months of symptom onset, but 19% delayed ≥12 weeks and 7.4% delayed ≥1 year (p1385).":["u11"],"copy|Biological behavior varies by tumor type and is not uniformly \"more indolent\" with age: malignant melanoma presents with more advanced, deeper local disease in older patients; lung cancer presents with more localized disease in older patients; breast cancer in older women is more often ER-positive with longer doubling time (generally better-prognosis biology) — yet overall cancer-related survival is still lower in older women (p1385).":["u12"],"copy|AML has a distinctly worse prognosis in older adults: poor-prognosis karyotypes (5q-, 7q-), antecedent MDS, chemo-resistance pathways, and lower apoptosis of leukemic blasts after ara-C/daunorubicin (p1385–1386).":["u13"],"cga-amp-chemotherapy-toxicity-risk-tools|CGA & Chemotherapy-Toxicity Risk Tools":["u14"],"copy|ECOG performance status (0 = perfect health, 5 = death) alone is not sufficient in geriatric oncology — a comprehensive geriatric assessment (CGA) adds substantial prognostic/functional information even in patients with a good ECOG PS (p1387–1388).":["u15"],"copy|CGA domains: functional status, comorbidities, nutrition, polypharmacy, psychological/cognitive status, socioeconomic issues, geriatric syndromes (p1388).":["u16"],"copy|CARG chemo-toxicity score: low risk 0–5 pts → 30% toxicity; intermediate 6–9 pts → 52%; high 10–19 pts → 83% (p1388).":["u17"],"copy|CRASH score: low-risk patients — 7% hematologic / 33% nonhematologic toxicity; high-risk patients — 100% hematologic / 93% nonhematologic toxicity (p1388).":["u18"],"copy|Full CGA tool: median completion time 22 minutes; 87% of patients complete their self-reported portion without assistance (p1388).":["u19"],"copy|CGA-guided intervention RCTs reduce treatment toxicity: cluster RCT across 41 practices, 718 patients → 20% absolute reduction in toxicity vs usual care; single-center MDT-CGA RCT in 620 patients → 10% reduction in chemo toxicity (p1388).":["u20"],"copy|Screening tools to select who needs a full CGA (Table 88-2, p1389): G8 (total score 17, ≤14 = geriatric risk present), Groningen Frailty Indicator (score 4–15 = risk present), VES-13 (score ≥3 = vulnerability), SAOP2, Abbreviated CGA (aCGA).":["u21"],"treatment-modalities|Treatment Modalities":["u22"],"copy|Surgery: healing is compromised at every phase (↓inflammatory/proliferative response, altered collagen); geriatric comanagement improves 90-day postoperative mortality (p1388–1389).":["u23"],"copy|Radiation: normal-tissue radiation effects may be enhanced by 10–15% in older patients; hypofractionated whole-breast RT is safe in older breast cancer; SBRT is an option when comorbidities preclude surgery for early NSCLC/pancreatic cancer; intermediate-risk prostate cancer may use 4–6 months hormonal therapy + EBRT (p1389).":["u24"],"copy|Chemotherapy: dose by creatinine clearance, not merely creatinine (↓lean body mass in older adults); watch H2 blockers/antacids/PPIs with oral agents; more common/severe in older adults — neurotoxicity (taxanes, platinum, vincristine, high-dose cytarabine, bortezomib), cardiotoxicity (doxorubicin), mucositis (5-FU) (p1390).":["u25"],"copy|Hormonal therapy: 70–80% of breast tumors in older patients are ER-positive. Tamoxifen ↑ thromboembolism/CVA risk — caution with venous insufficiency, HTN, AF. Aromatase inhibitors ↑ osteoporosis/fracture risk. Bone-directed therapy threshold: T-score < −2.0 SD alone, OR < −1.5 SD with one additional risk factor, OR two or more risk factors (without BMD) (p1390).":["u26"],"copy|Targeted therapy: mostly oral, CYP450-metabolized — high drug-interaction risk with polypharmacy; higher rate of dose reduction/discontinuation in older vs younger patients (p1390).":["u27"],"copy|Immunotherapy (ICIs): equivalent incidence of high-grade irAEs in older vs younger adults, but worse overall survival among older adults who develop high-grade irAEs — likely because steroids/hospitalization for irAE treatment are less well tolerated (p1391).":["u28"],"copy|HSCT: historically allogeneic SCT limited to age <50; autologous SCT now performed in fit patients >70. Delirium occurs in up to 50% of allogeneic HSCT recipients (p1391).":["u29"],"supportive-amp-palliative-care|Supportive & Palliative Care":["u30"],"copy|Routine symptom assessment/intervention can improve overall survival upwards of 5 months with no change in cancer-directed treatment (p1391).":["u31"],"copy|Pain: present in 50–90% of advanced disease, 60–70% of patients on active treatment; HELP study — one in three patients died in severe pain (p1392).":["u32"],"copy|Fatigue: prevalence 40–90% during treatment, 19–80% after treatment completion; exercise is one of the most effective treatments despite being counter-intuitive (p1392).":["u33"],"copy|Depression: prevalence 17–25% among cancer patients; SSRIs first-line, mirtazapine useful for mood/appetite/sleep, nortriptyline/desipramine third-line (p1392).":["u34"],"copy|End-of-life nutrition: IV fluids/TPN/tube feeding do not prolong life and may worsen comfort (pulmonary congestion, edema); dehydration near death is generally comfortable except for xerostomia (treat with swabs/ice chips) (p1392).":["u35"],"survivorship-amp-caregivers|Survivorship & Caregivers":["u36"],"copy|By 2030, 70% of cancer patients will be 65+, with incidence in this group rising ~70% from 2010–2030. The chapter counted 16 million US cancer survivors at the time of writing, ~60% age 65+; by 2040, projected 26 million survivors, 73% age 65+, almost 50% age 75+ (p1392–1393).":["u37"],"copy|63% of home care for older cancer patients comes from informal caregivers; nearly 40% of these caregivers have their own comorbidities and rate their own health fair/poor (p1393).":["u38"],"exam-traps-book-nuances|Exam traps / book nuances":["u39"],"copy|Do not equate \"more indolent tumor biology\" with \"better survival\" — older women have more ER-positive, longer-doubling-time breast cancers (favorable biology) yet still have lower overall cancer-related survival than younger women (p1385).":["u40"],"copy|Direction of stage-at-presentation with age is tumor-specific, not uniform: melanoma presents more advanced in older adults; lung cancer presents less advanced (more localized) in older adults — do not assume all cancers present later in older patients (p1385).":["u41"],"copy|CARG and CRASH are two different chemo-toxicity risk tools with different point scales and different endpoints (CARG = single composite risk tier; CRASH = separate hematologic/nonhematologic percentages) — don't merge their numbers (p1388).":["u42"],"recall|Recall":["u43"],"copy|CHAPTER 88s / HAZZARD":["u44"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 88; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u45"],"copy|1.":["u46"],"copy|What two summary statistics from the National Cancer Institute establish cancer as a disease of aging?":["u47"],"copy|Over 60% of newly diagnosed malignant tumors and 70% of all cancer deaths occur in persons 65 years or older (p1381).":["u48"],"copy|2.":["u49"],"copy|At what age does telomere shortening begin, and at what rate does it progress?":["u50"],"copy|Begins at age 30, progressing at a loss of approximately 1% per year (p1383).":["u51"],"copy|4.":["u52"],"copy|In the New Mexico study of 800 patients over 65, what fraction were asymptomatic at cancer detection, and what fraction delayed seeking care at least 1 year?":["u53"],"copy|Only 29% were asymptomatic at detection; 7.4% delayed seeking care for at least 1 year (p1385).":["u54"],"copy|5.":["u55"],"copy|Does malignant melanoma present at a more or less advanced stage in older patients, and how does this compare with lung cancer?":["u56"],"copy|Melanoma presents with more advanced-stage, deeper-penetrating local disease in older patients; lung cancer shows the opposite pattern, with older patients more often presenting with localized disease (p1385).":["u57"],"copy|6.":["u58"],"copy|Why does breast cancer tend to behave more indolently in older women biologically, and does this translate into better overall survival?":["u59"],"copy|Older women have a higher frequency of ER-positive tumors and longer tumor doubling times (both favorable prognostic features), but overall cancer-related survival is still lower in older women (p1385).":["u60"],"copy|8.":["u61"],"copy|On the CARG chemotherapy-toxicity risk model, what toxicity rates correspond to the low, intermediate, and high risk-point tiers?":["u62"],"copy|Low risk (0–5 points): 30% toxicity.":["u63"],"copy|Intermediate (6–9 points): 52%.":["u64"],"copy|High (10–19 points): 83% (p1388).":["u65"],"copy|9.":["u66"],"copy|On the CRASH score, what hematologic and nonhematologic toxicity rates were seen in high-risk versus low-risk patients?":["u67"],"copy|High-risk: 100% hematologic, 93% nonhematologic toxicity.":["u68"],"copy|Low-risk: 7% hematologic, 33% nonhematologic toxicity (p1388).":["u69"],"copy|12.":["u70"],"copy|What T-score thresholds trigger bone-directed therapy in an older patient on aromatase-inhibitor therapy?":["u71"],"copy|T-score less than −2.0 SD alone, OR less than −1.5 SD with one additional risk factor, OR two or more risk factors (without bone mineral density) (p1390).":["u72"],"copy|14.":["u73"],"copy|What proportion of allogeneic HSCT recipients develop delirium, and how does historical age eligibility for allogeneic versus autologous SCT differ?":["u74"],"copy|Up to 50% develop delirium.":["u75"],"copy|Allogeneic SCT was historically limited to patients under age 50, while autologous SCT has been performed in fit patients over age 70 (p1391).":["u76"],"copy|15.":["u77"],"copy|By 2040, what proportion of US cancer survivors are projected to be 65 or older, and 75 or older?":["u78"],"copy|73% are projected to be 65 or older, and almost 50% are projected to be 75 or older, out of a projected 26 million total survivors (p1393).":["u79"],"copy|Answer":["u80","u81","u82","u83","u84","u85","u86","u87","u88","u89"],"copy|Geriatric assessment reduced severe toxicity in two trials: in GAP70+ it fell from 71% to 51% with tailored recommendations for the oncologist, and in GAIN it fell from 60.6% to 50.5% with multidisciplinary implementation (p1388).":["u20"],"copy|Telomeres protect chromosome ends. Their progressive loss can destabilize chromosomes and increase mutation risk. Page 1383 describes a functional decline from age 30 at about 1% per year. Measured leukocyte telomere shortening also occurs in infancy, and its rate varies with age. Human leukocyte study":["u6"],"copy|ECOG performance status (0 = fully active without restriction from disease, 5 = death) alone is not sufficient in geriatric oncology — a comprehensive geriatric assessment (CGA) adds substantial prognostic/functional information even in patients with a good ECOG PS (p1387–1388).":["u15"],"copy|Surgery: healing is compromised at every phase (↓inflammatory/proliferative response, altered collagen); in a retrospective cohort of patients aged 75 or older undergoing cancer surgery, geriatric comanagement was associated with lower 90-day mortality (pp1388–1389). Original surgical cohort":["u23"],"copy|Immunotherapy (ICIs): one retrospective cohort observed similar frequencies of high-grade immune-related events by age. Among those with high-grade events, the older group had shorter median survival. Susceptibility to complications of steroids or hospitalization is one proposed explanation (p1391). Original cohort, Table 3":["u28"],"copy|In one trial of electronic symptom monitoring during treatment, median survival was 31.2 versus 26.0 months. A later community trial found better symptom control, function and quality of life without a survival difference. Symptom monitoring can help even when survival is unchanged (p1391). Original trial · PRO-TECT":["u31"],"copy|In the last days of life with advanced cancer, routine artificial hydration or nutrition has no established survival benefit and can add edema, pulmonary congestion or procedural burden. Ask about thirst and discomfort, offer mouth care and safe sips or ice chips as tolerated, and individualize decisions with the patient and family (p1392). NCI, Last Days of Life":["u35"],"copy|How can loss of telomere function promote malignancy, and when has telomere shortening been measured?":["u50"],"copy|Loss of chromosome-end protection can promote genomic instability.":["u51"],"copy|The chapter describes functional decline from age 30 at about 1% per year (p1383).":["u51"],"copy|Leukocyte measurements show that shortening is already present in early childhood and proceeds at an age-dependent rate.":["u51"]},"maxId":89}
